# Neuroscience Research Data Pipeline

A dynamic data center built from global scientific literature on neuroscience. The
pipeline discovers papers across multiple open sources, extracts and chunks their full
text, and embeds those chunks into a vector store — serving both a semantic-search / RAG
index and a versioned training dataset. It is designed to keep growing as the field
evolves and as new sources are added.

## Directory Structure

```text
research-<topic>/
├── README                    # Project documentation
├── run_pipeline.sh           # Orchestrates ingest -> extract -> chunk -> embed -> report
├── requirements.txt          # Python dependencies
├── core/                     # Pipeline package (one module per stage)
│   ├── config.py             # TOPIC autodetect, paths, EMAIL_CONTACT, KEYWORD_BLOCKS, model
│   ├── registry.py           # SQLite control plane (papers + cursors, status FSM, dedup)
│   ├── log.py                # Logging setup
│   ├── sources/              # openalex.py, europepmc.py, crossref.py, resolvers.py
│   ├── ingest.py             # discover -> dedup -> resolve -> fetch -> register
│   ├── extract.py            # PDF -> Markdown (fitz first, Marker fallback, quality score)
│   ├── chunk.py              # Markdown -> section-aware chunks
│   ├── encoders.py           # Text encoder (bge-m3), batched, device auto
│   ├── embed.py              # Chunks -> embeddings -> LanceDB
│   ├── store.py              # LanceDB chunk table: search + parquet export
│   ├── report.py             # QC statistics
│   └── purge.py              # Raw-PDF cleanup (--min-quality retention)
└── storage/                  # Multi-tier data lake
    ├── 1_raw_data/           # Temporary landing zone for source PDFs
    ├── 2_register_data/      # SQLite registry (<topic>_registry.db)
    ├── 3_exploitable_data/   # Clean normalized text (Markdown)
    └── 4_vector_data/        # Chunk JSON + LanceDB table (vectors + metadata)
```

## Naming & Directory Conventions

- **Root directory rule**: the root must follow `research-xxx` (e.g. `research-neurosciences`).
- **Dynamic registry mapping**: the `xxx` suffix is extracted at runtime to instantiate the
  ledger at `storage/2_register_data/xxx_registry.db`. No manual configuration when cloning
  this architecture for a new topic.
- **Fixed data-lake hierarchy**: the folder names inside `storage/` are locked and immutable.

## Sources

Discovery runs across several complementary open sources; a shared resolver cascade turns
each discovered paper into a downloadable PDF or scraped HTML.

| Source | Role | How it is handled |
|---|---|---|
| OpenAlex | Primary discovery | `core.sources.openalex`, boolean keyword blocks, polite pool via `EMAIL_CONTACT` |
| Europe PMC | Discovery (biomedical, OA full text) | `core.sources.europepmc`, `cursorMark` pagination |
| Crossref | Discovery (broad DOI coverage) | `core.sources.crossref`, feeds the resolver cascade |
| Unpaywall / Semantic Scholar / CORE | PDF resolution | `core.sources.resolvers`, fallback cascade after direct arXiv/PMC candidates |

`sources/` stays pluggable — arXiv/bioRxiv can be added later behind the same `discover()`
contract. Set a real contact address with `export NEURO_CONTACT_EMAIL=you@example.org`
(OpenAlex and Unpaywall throttle empty contacts).

### Query breadth

`config.KEYWORD_BLOCKS` covers neuroscience broadly (cognitive, systems/circuits,
molecular/cellular, computational, neuroimaging, clinical neurology, neurodegeneration,
psychiatry, addiction, developmental/aging, sensory systems, sleep/circadian,
neuropharmacology, glia/neuroimmunology, neuromodulation, BCI, electrophysiology, and a
wearable/ear-EEG cluster) to maximize corpus size. The blocks are freely editable.

## Data Lifecycle

The SQLite registry tracks each paper through an explicit status machine. Papers are
deduplicated across sources by canonical DOI, normalized title, and — after extraction —
the SHA-256 hash of the Markdown body (near-duplicate full text).

```
discovered -> fetched -> extracted -> chunked -> embedded
    └-> paywalled | empty | duplicate | failed   (terminal)
```

1. **Ingestion** (`core.ingest`): discovers papers per source × query block (pagination
   cursors persisted per source × block), dedups, resolves an open-access URL, and fetches
   a PDF (`fetched`) or scrapes HTML into Markdown (`extracted`). No OA source resolved →
   `paywalled`, with the abstract retained.
2. **Extraction** (`core.extract`): fitz first; escalates to Marker's vision model only when
   the fitz quality score is low and Marker is installed. Writes Markdown with front-matter
   to Tier 3. Identical full text already seen → `duplicate`.
3. **Chunking** (`core.chunk`): splits Markdown into section-aware, metadata-rich chunks
   (one JSON per paper) in Tier 4.
4. **Embedding** (`core.embed`): batch-encodes all chunks (`bge-m3`) and upserts one row per
   chunk into the LanceDB table — the RAG index. The table exports to Parquet for a dataset.
5. **Reporting / Purge** (`core.report`, `core.purge`): QC statistics; and raw-PDF cleanup
   that only ever touches Tier 1.

## How to Run

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
export NEURO_CONTACT_EMAIL=you@example.org   # OpenAlex polite pool + Unpaywall
```

Stages run as package modules from the project root:

```bash
python -m core.ingest --limit 50        # discover + fetch (all sources, or --source openalex,crossref)
python -m core.extract                  # PDF -> Markdown
python -m core.chunk                    # Markdown -> chunks
python -m core.embed                    # chunks -> LanceDB
python -m core.report                   # QC stats
python -m core.store --export out.parquet   # dataset export
python -m core.purge --dry-run          # then --yes / --min-quality 0.5
```

### All in One

```bash
./run_pipeline.sh                       # optional: --source, --limit N, --purge, --yes
```

`marker-pdf` is optional: without it, extraction runs on fitz alone.
